{"content":{"blocks":[{"text":"Reverted. To answer directly: I had built the auto-PR — the option you suspected was half-baked — and you were right to push back.\n\n**What's on the branch now:**\n\nThe workflow is back to `permissions: contents: read` with no repo writes of any kind. Jobs are `check`, `file-issue`, and `close-issue`. Verified: 77/77 tests pass, drift check exits 0 with both targets validated, YAML parses, and there's no `contents: write` or `pull-requests` permission anywhere in the file.\n\nI kept two pieces that stood on their own rather than existing to serve the PR job:\n\n- **The issue now closes itself** when drift clears. That was a real gap — nothing ever closed it, so #2 would have sat open indefinitely after this merges.\n- **The issue's checklist names the headless probes**, not just `smoke:interactive`. The original three-step list would have had you widen a pin on the strength of the TUI render check alone.\n\nI dropped the `--fix` flag along with the PR job. It existed to feed CI; locally it's a one-line edit, and keeping ~40 lines of prefix-rewriting logic to save that isn't worth the maintenance.\n\nThe reasoning is recorded in the workflow header and the roadmap rather than just in this conversation, since \"why not just auto-draft the PR?\" is exactly the kind of idea that gets rebuilt from scratch in six months. Both commits are on the branch — the build and the revert — so the record shows the thing was tried rather than merely considered.\n\nThe revalidation work itself is untouched: pin widened to `0.146.`, spec §10 evidence, version 0.5.2. PR #4 is still a draft with an updated title and body.\n\nresult:\nReverted the auto-drafted PR — drift automation stays reporting-only (no repo write permissions), keeping issue auto-close and a probe-inclusive checklist; codex 0.146.0 revalidation unchanged on draft PR #4.","type":"text"}],"role":"assistant"},"context":{"branch":"worktree-codex-0146-revalidation","cwd":"/Users/evintunador/